# Normalise line endings.
#
# Every tracked file in this repo is stored with LF. Without this file,
# Git on Windows rewrites working copies to CRLF on checkout, which has
# two visible costs, both seen repeatedly on 2026-09-04:
#
#   1. `git add` prints "LF will be replaced by CRLF" for every file.
#   2. Whole files show as modified in `git status` when nothing in them
#      changed, which hides real edits in the noise.
#
# eol=lf rather than a bare `text=auto`: this repo is also checked out on
# Linux CI runners. (The node_modules/ ones are ignored.)
#
# After adding this, run once:  git add --renormalize .
* text=auto eol=lf

# ...with one exception, added 2026-09-11. The note above used to say
# "no tracked file is a .bat/.cmd/.ps1 that would need CRLF to work".
# archive_daily.cmd is now exactly that file: the wrapper Task Scheduler
# runs for the daily archive. cmd.exe is not reliably tolerant of LF-only
# batch files - parenthesised `if` blocks and delayed expansion, which
# that script uses throughout, are the constructs that break - and the
# failure is a scheduled job that stops archiving without saying so,
# which is the failure mode the whole archive exists to prevent.
#
# Windows scripts are stored with CRLF and checked out with CRLF. They
# are never run on the Linux runners.
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Binary - never convert, never diff as text.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.db    binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.tif   binary
*.tiff  binary
*.jp2   binary
